## Router / intent misfires

Four entries share one root cause: intent routing in `daemon/router.py` is
regex-first (`COMPOSE_HINT` and friends), so keyword hits win over what the
user actually asked for. Fix these as a cluster — every one below should end
up as a regression test in the same suite.

### 1. "email" anywhere in a prompt hijacks into compose (todo #11) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** COMPOSE_HINT now needs an explicit write verb (bare "email" only
  as an imperative); read-shaped requests route to the tool loop via
  MAIL_READ_HINT (search_email). Live-verified: "look through my email…"
  searched and answered, no popup.
- **Observed:** "look through my email for X" → opens an empty compose popup
  and does nothing else.
- **Root cause:** `COMPOSE_HINT` matches email-ish phrasing regardless of
  intent; there is no read/search intent that outranks it.
- **Fix:** compose should require an explicit write verb (send / draft /
  reply / write an email **to**). Read verbs (look through, find, search,
  check, read) must route to inbox search/QA even when "email" appears.

### 2. Multi-step email tasks collapse into one wrong tool call (todo #9) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** find-then-send special case — draft carries a `to_hint` (with a
  deterministic "<Name>'s address" fallback, since the 4B often omits it);
  the mirror lookup prefills the newest matching sender. Live-verified with
  the exact acceptance prompt: compose opened with to + body prefilled.
- **Observed:** "find Chris Sizemore's address from his most recent email,
  then send him an email with body '…'" → empty compose popup.
- **Root cause:** the router dispatches exactly one intent per prompt; there
  is no plan-then-act step, so the compose path never receives the search
  result it depends on.
- **Fix:** support short tool chains — search → extract recipient → open
  compose **prefilled** (to, subject, body), still behind the existing send
  confirmation. Either let the model emit an ordered tool list, or
  special-case the common "find-then-send" shape first. Use the exact prompt
  above as the acceptance test.

### 3. Markdown-file request routed to calendar event (todo #15) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** FILE_TASK_HINT guards the event branches; file requests reach the
  fs tool loop (write_file exists there, behind the write gate). Live-verified:
  "create a meeting note file…" ran fs tools + write-gate confirm, no event
  dialog. The dedicated file-writing intent remains `new-features.md` item 2.
- **Observed:** asked Lumen to write a markdown file → it opened a calendar
  event instead.
- **Root cause:** no file-writing tool exists (see `new-features.md`
  backlog item 2), so the nearest greedy heuristic won.
- **Fix:** when no capable tool exists, Lumen must say "I can't do that yet"
  instead of firing the closest match; tighten the event-create heuristic to
  require event-ish language. Fully resolved once the file-writing feature
  lands.

### 4. Tools never load after a no-tool first turn (todo #6) — ✔ verified + regression-tested
- **Done:** the route decision was already strictly per-turn in current code;
  added the two-turn regression tests (calendar + files shapes) and live-ran
  the exact scenario — the second turn loaded tools and answered from real
  calendar data.
- **Observed:** first turn is a plain question (no tools — fine), but a later
  turn in the same chat that needs a tool doesn't load one either.
- **Root hypothesis:** the tool-vs-direct decision is sticky per
  conversation, or the prior direct-answer turn in history biases routing.
- **Fix:** make the route decision strictly per-turn, independent of how
  earlier turns were routed. Regression test: no-tool turn, then "what's on
  my calendar tomorrow" in the same chat.

## Chat sessions

Entries 5 and 6 are two sides of the same broken session model — fix
together, in `ui_v2/state.py` + `screens/chat.py` + the daemon's history
assembly.

### 5. Every prompt creates a new chat (todo #8) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** one `state.active_conv_id` shared by launcher palette, hotkey
  overlay, and Chat screen; prompts append to it, "New chat" (or first-ever
  prompt) is the only reset. Surfaces re-sync on show.
- **Observed:** each typed prompt lands in a fresh chat instead of
  continuing the open one.
- **Fix:** one active chat id in UI state; prompts append to it. A new chat
  is created only by an explicit "new chat" action (or first-ever prompt).

### 6. New chats don't get fresh context (todo #10) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** daemon history was already keyed by conversation id; both
  directions now regression-tested (continuing keeps context, new chat knows
  nothing). The visible bleed was the launcher reusing one thread forever —
  gone with the shared active id.
- **Observed:** context appears to be one blob per daemon session — old
  chats bleed into new ones.
- **Fix:** history sent to the model must be keyed by chat id and contain
  only that chat's turns. "New chat" = genuinely empty context.
- **Test both directions:** continuing a chat keeps its context; a new chat
  knows nothing from the previous one.

## Model serving

### 7. Cold start on every launcher prompt (todo #2) — ✔ mitigation verified, iGPU fix still open
- **Done:** the interim mitigation was already built — overlay summon and
  launcher/chat tab switches send `warm` (identity+memory KV prime);
  live-verified it hits Ollama. The Vulkan/iGPU move stays a feature item.
- **Observed:** prompting from the launcher always pays the full model load.
- **Known direction (already decided):** move serving to the iGPU via Vulkan
  (llama.cpp) so a resident/fast-loading model is cheap enough thermally to
  keep warm longer.
- **Interim mitigation:** when the launcher is summoned (hotkey), start
  loading the model in the background while the user types — typing time
  hides most of the load. Idle-unload stays; this changes *when* loading
  starts, not whether we unload.

## Calendar

### 8. Relative dates resolve wrong on event creation (todo #1) — ✔ fixed
- **Done:** `resolve_relative_phrase` (todo_parse.py) resolves weekdays,
  "next X", "next week on X", "in N days/weeks" deterministically; the
  resolved date is injected into the prompt AND mechanically overrides the
  model's date (`apply_resolved_date`). Booking-context follow-ups exempt.
  Live-verified: "next wednesday at 3pm" on a Friday → Wed Jul 22 confirm.
- **Observed:** "create X event next wednesday" / "next week on wednesday"
  → wrong date on the created event.
- **Root cause:** date math is left to the small local model
  (`daemon/llm/event_create.py`) — small models are unreliable at calendar
  arithmetic.
- **Follow-up 2026-07-19 — month/year arithmetic was still missing.**
  `resolve_relative_phrase` handled weekdays and day/week offsets but had no
  month or year patterns at all, so "March next year" reached the 4B
  unresolved. Measured against the live model (2 trials each): explicit
  "next year" phrasings it actually gets right, but a YEAR-LESS month name
  whose month has already passed comes back with the CURRENT year 6/6 times —
  "the conference in March", said in July 2026, proposes March 2026, which the
  gate then rejects as "that date is in the past". So the user-visible symptom
  was a flat refusal, not just a wrong date.
  **Done:** month/day/year patterns added (`march 5th next year`, `3rd of
  december`, `in 3 months`, `next month`, literal years), plus
  `correct_year` — an explicit year phrase wins outright, and otherwise a
  year-less month that landed in the past rolls forward one year. Ambiguous
  month words ("may" the modal, "march" the verb) need a date cue, so "you may
  5 minutes late" is not May 5th. Live-verified through the daemon:
  "in March next year" → Mon Mar 15 2027; "on my calendar in March" →
  Tue Mar 23 2027 (model said 2026).
- **Fix:** resolve relative dates deterministically in code (extend the
  `todo_parse.py` patterns: weekday names, "next X", "next week on X",
  "in N days") and hand the model a concrete date; also inject "today is
  <date> (<weekday>)" into the prompt as a backstop. Unit-test the edge
  cases: "next wednesday" said on a Wednesday, month/year rollover.

