  ## 15. The HTML rendering needs some serious improvements, currently the emails with HTML sometimes look okay other times it looks horrible — ✔ fixed
  - **Root cause:** the sender's HTML went to QTextBrowser untouched. It renders
    only a subset of HTML4/CSS2, so what "sometimes looked okay" was whichever
    half of the sender's CSS happened to apply. Two failure modes dominated:
    a `bgcolor`/dark palette overrode the white paper card and then any dark
    text on it went invisible, and fixed pixel widths (900px+ table shells)
    overflowed a pane whose horizontal scrollbar is off, cutting content.
  - **Done:** `mail_html.prepare_html` normalizes before rendering — `<script>`
    /`<style>`/`<head>` blocks dropped (half-applied CSS is the problem, not the
    solution), `bgcolor` dropped, text colours too pale to read on the card
    dropped so they inherit the card's, pixel widths over the pane width
    dropped, and a sane base font/line-height wrapped around the result.
    Screenshot-verified before/after on a dark-palette statement email: before,
    a dark card with an invisible account line; after, one readable paper card.

  ## 16. Do not render emails with no HTML like they are html — ✔ fixed
  - **Root cause:** plain-text bodies went to a bare `QLabel`, whose default
    `Qt::AutoText` runs `mightBeRichText()` and renders anything tag-shaped as
    HTML. Live-probed: "a < b and c > d" renders as "a d" — the reading pane was
    silently eating message content. "5 &lt; 6 &amp; 7" and any `<br>`/`<p>`
    mentioned in prose hit the same path.
  - **Done:** plain bodies never reach a rich-text guess. `mail_html.plain_to_html`
    escapes FIRST, then emits markup it chose itself; the pane renders that.

  ## 17. Make emails look more like normal email readers when not using HTML — ✔ fixed
  - **Done:** plain text now lands on the same white paper card as HTML mail
    instead of a bare dark-background label. `plain_to_html` keeps hard line
    breaks, spaces paragraphs, indents and dims `>` quoted passages by depth
    (the `>` markers themselves drop out, as every real reader does), dims the
    signature after `--`, and linkifies URLs and bare addresses.
    Screenshot-verified.

  ## 18. Why is it a bad idea to always load the images for emails? — ✔ answered + always-load shipped
  - **The answer:** remote images are the standard read-receipt mechanism. A 1x1
    "tracking pixel" with a URL unique to you means fetching it tells the sender
    you opened the message, when, how many times, and the IP it came from
    (rough location + ISP) — and confirms the address is live, which attracts
    more mail. That is the whole cost; it is real but modest, and Gmail already
    fetches these for you through Google's proxy today.
  - **Decision (user, 2026-07-19): always load.** Implemented as
    `[mail] load_remote_images = true` (daemon config, shown in Settings).
    The reading pane fetches on open, off the GUI thread, once per message, and
    repaints; the stripped view shows meanwhile so text is readable immediately.
    Setting it false restores the click-to-load bar, which is still tested.

  ## 19. When I asked lumen to add something to my todo list it tried to write to a file called TODO, rather than add it to the todo list integrated in the Lumen Application — ✔ fixed
  - **Root cause (a capability gap, not a routing miss):** todos had NO tools at
    all. The model could read the open list from injected context but had no way
    to act on it, so every todo write depended on the TODO_ADD/MARK_DONE regexes
    matching first. "add buy milk to my todo list" misses TODO_ADD, and TOOL_HINT
    matches the "list" in "todo list" — so the request arrived carrying the
    FILESYSTEM group and nothing that could touch a todo. The model picked the
    nearest capable tool and wrote a file. Same class as #3.
  - **Done:** todos are a real tool group. `daemon/local_tools.py` exposes
    add_todo / complete_todo / list_todos in-process (the daemon owns the SQLite
    handle; a fourth MCP server would mean a second writer for no benefit), the
    router attaches them whenever the todo subject matter is present, and
    todo_context now carries ids plus an explicit "never by writing a file".
    Deletion stays a UI action. Live-verified: "add buy milk to my todo list"
    called add_todo, the row landed in the real list with source=chat, and no
    TODO file was created anywhere under $HOME.

